    "evidence_text": "Ancient-Modern Chinese Translation with a Large Training Dataset\tAncient Chinese brings the wisdom and spirit culture of the Chinese nation.\n\nHowever, the lack of large-scale parallel corpus limits the study of machine translation in Ancient-Modern Chinese.\n\nIn this paper, we propose an Ancient-Modern Chinese clause alignment approach based on the characteristics of these two languages.\n\nTo our best knowledge, this is the first large high-quality Ancient-Modern Chinese dataset.\n\nIntroduction\tAncient Chinese is the writing language in ancient China.\n\nLearning ancient Chinese not only helps people to understand and inherit the wisdom of the ancients, but\n\nalso promotes people to absorb and develop Chinese culture.\n\nHowever, it is difficult for modern people to read ancient Chinese.\n\nFirstly, compared with modern Chinese, ancient Chinese is more concise and shorter.\n\nThe grammatical order of modern Chinese is also quite different from that of ancient Chinese.\n\nThirdly, there is more than one polysemous phenomenon in ancient Chinese.\n\nTherefore, it is meaningful and useful to study the automatic translation from ancient Chinese to modern Chinese.\n\nHowever, NMT approach has not been widely applied to the ancient-modern Chinese translation task.\n\nOne of the main reasons is the limited high-quality parallel data resource.\n\nThe most popular method of acquiring translation examples is bilingual text alignment  .\n\nmethod can be classified into two types: lexical-based and statistical-based.\n\nThe ancient-modern Chinese has some characteristics that are quite different from other language pairs.\n\nThe traditional methods do not take these characteristics into account.\n\nRecently, a simple longest common subsequence based approach for ancient-modern Chinese sentence alignment is proposed in  .\n\nWe apply the proposed method to create a large\n\nTo our best knowledge, this is the first large high-quality ancient-modern Chinese dataset.\n\nOverview\tThere are four steps to build the ancient-modern Chinese translation dataset: (i) The parallel corpus crawling and cleaning.\n\n(iv) Augmenting data by merging aligned adjacent clauses.\n\nThe lexical matching score is used to calculate the matching coverage of the ancient clause INLINEFORM0 .\n\nAn ancient Chinese character usually corresponds to one or more modern Chinese words.\n\nIn the\n\nfirst part, we carry out Chinese Word segmentation to the modern Chinese clause INLINEFORM1 .\n\nThen we match the ancient characters and modern words in the order from left to right.\n\nHowever, some ancient characters do not appear in its corresponding modern Chinese words.\n\nAn ancient Chinese dictionary is employed to address this issue.\n\nWe preprocess the ancient Chinese dictionary and remove the stop words.\n\nThe second term is dictionary matching score.\n\nHere INLINEFORM8 and INLINEFORM9 represent the remaining unmatched\n\nStatistical Information.\n\nThere are many alignment modes between ancient and modern Chinese languages.\n\nEdit Distance.\n\nBecause ancient and modern Chinese are both written in Chinese characters, we also consider using the edit distance.\n\nWe use dynamic programming to find the overall optimal alignment paragraph by paragraph.\n\nAncient-Modern Chinese Dataset\tData Collection.\n\nTo build the large ancient-modern Chinese dataset, we collected 1.7K bilingual ancient-modern Chinese articles from the internet.\n\nAfter data cleaning and manual paragraph alignment, we obtained 35K aligned bilingual paragraphs.\n\nClause Alignment.\n\nTo the best of our knowledge,  is the latest related work which are designed for Ancient-Modern Chinese alignment.\n\nHyper-parameters.\n\nThe Jieba Chinese text segmentation is employed for modern Chinese word segmentation.\n\nResults.\n\n94.2 F1-score on Test set.\n\nTo our best knowledge, this is the first large high-quality ancient-modern Chinese dataset.",
